FINDING
-------
The float64 reference is bit-identical to the int64 reference at every shape checked --
the three sweep shapes, the full-range worst case and the all -128 bound case at K = 4096 --
and 15-2182x faster (532.74 s -> 0.244 s at 2048x4096x4096). The patched whole_array.py
still prints PASS! on the same data the 2026-09-07 sweep passed with the int64 oracle, so
every PASS! in results/aie/int8_matmul_sweep_npu.log and
gemm_tile_sweep_c_single_buffer_npu.log stands unchanged in meaning. The 4096-class int8
rows now cost seconds of wall instead of minutes, which is what made the int8 sweeps take
longer than the bf16 ones. The CPU sweep script's check is now exact at every shape instead
of skipped above 2^30 MACs. Nothing measured about the NPU moved.

NOT TESTED
----------
- int16 inputs (bound K <= 2^23; the sweeps only run int8 and bf16).
- The int64 fallback branch (no shape here exceeds the bound).
- A clean-CPU timing of either reference: another session's yolov6n evaluations ran during
  check_ref.py (see "Contention, stated" above).
